The "break" keyword in the C programming language is used to exit or terminate a loop, prematurely ending its execution. When encountered within a loop, the "break" statement jumps out of the loop's block, allowing the program to continue executing the next statement after the loop.

On the other hand, the "continue" keyword is used to skip the rest of the current iteration of a loop and proceed to the next iteration. When the "continue" statement is encountered, the program will jump to the loop's condition check, skipping any code below the "continue" statement for that particular iteration.

Here's an example to demonstrate the use of both keywords within a loop to manipulate an array:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
   int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
   int i;

   printf("Modified array: ");
   for (i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
      if (arr[i] == 3)
         break;  // terminates the loop when the value is 3

      if (arr[i] == 2)
         continue;  // skips the rest of the current iteration when the value is 2

      printf("%d ", arr[i]);
   }

   return 0;
}
```

In this example, the loop iterates through the array "arr" and prints its elements, except when the value is 3. When the loop encounters the value 3, the "break" statement is executed, terminating the loop immediately. Similarly, when the loop encounters the value 2, the "continue" statement is executed, skipping the remaining code for that iteration and proceeding to the next iteration.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n), where n is the size of the array. The loop iterates through all the elements of the array once. The space complexity is O(1) as no additional space is used apart from the array itself and the loop variable "i".

