The time complexity of an algorithm and the formula for its return value are often different. This is because the time complexity represents the time taken to execute the algorithm, while the return value represents the output of the algorithm.

For example, in the factorial function:

```
int factorial(int n) {
  if(n==0) return 1;
  else return n*factorial(n-1);
}
```

The recurrence relation for the return value is T(n) = nT(n-1), which gives a time complexity of O(n!). However, the time complexity of the algorithm is actually O(n), because the multiplication by n does not increase the time complexity by a factor of n. Instead, the recurrence relation for the time complexity is T(n) = T(n-1) + c, where 'c' is a constant representing the time for multiplication.

