Here is a Java program that takes an array of strings as input and prints out each element in reverse order using different functions:

```java
public class ReverseStringArray {

    public static void reverseStringArray(String[] arr) {
        int start = 0;
        int end = arr.length - 1;

        while (start < end) {
            swap(arr, start, end);
            start++;
            end--;
        }
    }

    private static void swap(String[] arr, int i, int j) {
        String temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = temp;
    }

    public static void printStringArray(String[] arr) {
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            System.out.println(arr[i]);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] arr = {"Hello", "World", "Java", "Program"};
        System.out.println("Original Array:");
        printStringArray(arr);

        System.out.println("Array in Reverse Order:");
        reverseStringArray(arr);
        printStringArray(arr);
    }
}
```

In this program, we have two functions: `reverseStringArray` and `printStringArray`. 

The `reverseStringArray` function uses a two-pointer technique to swap the elements in the array in reverse order. It starts with the first and last elements of the array, swaps them, and then moves the pointers towards the center until they meet. This is done using the `swap` function.

The `printStringArray` function simply loops through the array and prints each element on a new line.

In the `main` method, we create an array of strings and print it in its original order. Then, we reverse the array using the `reverseStringArray` function and print it in reverse order.

